WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:07.504
Outside of Philadelphia,

00:00:07.504 --> 00:00:12.634
there is an old nursing home
that peaks out from behind lush trees.

00:00:13.677 --> 00:00:19.724
Instead of caring for the old,
these days, it detains the young.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.604
Kids who came to the United States
with their parents.

00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:27.399
Inside its hallways,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.111
a little boy named Diego
went from diapers to detention.

00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:33.905
When he was just one year old,

00:00:33.947 --> 00:00:38.410
Diego's mother, Wendy, decided
that life in Honduras was too dangerous

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.786
for them to stay.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.999
Like people from all over the world
have done for generations,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.669
Diego and Wendy turned
to the United States for safety.

00:00:48.503 --> 00:00:54.467
If you are in the United States
and you are afraid for your life,

00:00:54.467 --> 00:01:00.015
federal law is clear
that you can ask for asylum.

00:01:00.849 --> 00:01:05.270
It does not matter where you came from
or how you got here.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:08.481
Diego and Wendy did just that.

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:10.317
Within a few days,

00:01:10.358 --> 00:01:14.154
they found themselves inside
that Pennsylvania immigration prison.

00:01:14.154 --> 00:01:18.033
Two among half a million people

00:01:18.074 --> 00:01:21.286
who will be locked up every single year,

00:01:21.328 --> 00:01:22.954
while the government decides

00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:26.291
if they will be allowed to stay
in the United States.

00:01:27.542 --> 00:01:29.127
Instead of a fair match

00:01:29.127 --> 00:01:33.590
between a prosecutor
and a defense attorney,

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.719
imprisoned migrants
usually walk into court alone.

00:01:38.386 --> 00:01:40.180
It's a high-stakes legal battle

00:01:40.221 --> 00:01:44.225
that they are forced to fight
with their hands tied behind their backs.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.648
As the courts deliberate, the days pass.

00:01:49.648 --> 00:01:53.485
From your community to mine,
today, someone is locked up

00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:56.154
who has not been accused of any crime.

00:01:56.196 --> 00:02:00.784
Often, they haven't seen a judge
or even a lawyer.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.371
And yet, in most immigration prisons

00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:09.125
that I have visited
as a lawyer or a researcher,

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:14.089
the steel door is closed shut
with the clink of confinement.

00:02:14.506 --> 00:02:16.257
For the sake of votes,

00:02:16.257 --> 00:02:22.180
politicians claim people like Diego
and Wendy are dangerous, or dishonest.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.100
For the sake of profits,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.687
private corporations
run prisons to house them.

00:02:30.438 --> 00:02:32.857
It seems unimaginable today,

00:02:32.899 --> 00:02:36.361
but we haven't always locked up migrants

00:02:36.361 --> 00:02:39.823
who are waiting for the government
to decide their fate.

00:02:40.824 --> 00:02:42.325
At one time,

00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:47.455
the United States stood on the verge
of abolishing immigration prisons.

00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:52.335
"This was the sign
of an enlightened civilization,"

00:02:52.377 --> 00:02:55.630
the Supreme Court wrote in 1958.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.216
We were so close.

00:02:58.842 --> 00:03:04.514
But politics and profits pushed us
in the opposite direction.

00:03:05.181 --> 00:03:09.060
With the support
of Republicans and Democrats,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:14.858
today, we lock up men,
we lock up women, and we lock up children.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.280
But in our past,
I see hope for the future.

00:03:21.322 --> 00:03:27.120
We can take clear steps
toward abolishing immigration prisons.

00:03:29.706 --> 00:03:32.417
Most people think of Ellis Island

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:37.297
as the place that welcomed generations
of newcomers to the United States.

00:03:38.089 --> 00:03:39.424
It did that,

00:03:39.466 --> 00:03:43.136
but it was also an immigration prison

00:03:43.136 --> 00:03:46.055
with an ironic view
of the Statue of Liberty.

00:03:47.348 --> 00:03:52.187
By the 1950s, the Ellis Island facility
needed to be repaired,

00:03:52.228 --> 00:03:53.897
replaced or discarded.

00:03:54.647 --> 00:03:58.818
The government of the war hero
turned Republican president,

00:03:58.818 --> 00:04:00.612
Dwight Eisenhower,

00:04:00.612 --> 00:04:02.405
decided to shut it down.

00:04:03.573 --> 00:04:08.077
But then, starting in the late 1970s,

00:04:08.119 --> 00:04:13.833
the United States built the largest
immigration prison system in the world.

00:04:14.793 --> 00:04:19.923
Republicans and Democrats worked together,

00:04:19.923 --> 00:04:26.179
pointing to the prison's barbed wire
to uphold the law to protect you ...

00:04:26.971 --> 00:04:28.139
from me.

00:04:28.765 --> 00:04:33.019
When Haitians started arriving
in large numbers in the 1970s,

00:04:33.061 --> 00:04:35.313
Carter turned to detention.

00:04:35.688 --> 00:04:42.529
In the 1980s, Reagan followed
by jailing Cubans and Central Americans.

00:04:43.613 --> 00:04:49.035
George H. W. Bush turned
to the military base at Guantanamo, Cuba,

00:04:49.077 --> 00:04:50.453
to jail migrants.

00:04:51.079 --> 00:04:53.832
President Clinton left them there.

00:04:54.249 --> 00:04:56.376
This isn't a partisan issue.

00:04:57.043 --> 00:04:59.546
This is a profits issue.

00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:03.174
To local governments
and private businesses,

00:05:03.174 --> 00:05:06.052
immigration prisons
are a financial spigot.

00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:10.390
The two largest private prison
corporations in the United States,

00:05:10.431 --> 00:05:12.725
CoreCivic and the GEO Group,

00:05:12.725 --> 00:05:15.562
get about half of their money
from the federal government.

00:05:15.895 --> 00:05:19.649
With that, they hire people
in out-of-the-way locations,

00:05:19.691 --> 00:05:22.610
where decent-paying jobs
are hard to come by.

00:05:23.236 --> 00:05:27.031
Down in South Texas,
head north from the border,

00:05:27.031 --> 00:05:32.287
and eventually, you will catch
the sharp stench of onions

00:05:32.287 --> 00:05:33.580
filling the air.

00:05:33.955 --> 00:05:36.708
The local economy of Raymondville --

00:05:36.749 --> 00:05:39.544
that's the county seat
of Willacy County --

00:05:39.586 --> 00:05:41.212
it runs on produce --

00:05:41.212 --> 00:05:44.716
on produce and on prisons.

00:05:44.757 --> 00:05:46.593
Tucked behind a state jail,

00:05:46.593 --> 00:05:52.098
the Willacy County Detention Center
houses federal immigration prisoners

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.017
in large canvas tents.

00:05:54.934 --> 00:05:57.520
A few years ago, this facility shut down,

00:05:57.562 --> 00:05:59.230
after inmates rebelled.

00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:02.734
And when that happened,
I was not surprised,

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:05.862
because back when I used
to represent clients there,

00:06:05.904 --> 00:06:09.741
stories of rape, harassment
and abuse were common.

00:06:10.742 --> 00:06:14.162
Eventually, the county
lost a few hundred jobs.

00:06:14.746 --> 00:06:16.956
Even the Walmart shut down.

00:06:18.082 --> 00:06:22.211
But then, in the summer of 2018,

00:06:22.211 --> 00:06:26.591
when the refurbished prison
was ramping up to reopen,

00:06:26.633 --> 00:06:29.510
the county's elected officials celebrated.

00:06:30.303 --> 00:06:32.639
Why was the county so interested?

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:36.267
It owned the prison,
and the private company just ran it.

00:06:36.267 --> 00:06:37.936
Having prisoners on the inside

00:06:37.936 --> 00:06:40.688
meant that the private company
could hire guards,

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:42.565
and it could hire nurses,

00:06:42.607 --> 00:06:45.652
and it meant that the county
could pay its bills.

00:06:47.278 --> 00:06:52.700
With support from Washington
down to Willacy County,

00:06:52.742 --> 00:06:55.954
it's a good time to be in the business
of locking up migrants.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:03.878
But we can't forget that these are people
who we're talking about,

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:10.009
from kids who are too young
to be asked their opinions,

00:07:10.051 --> 00:07:14.055
to adults with longtime ties
to the United States.

00:07:15.515 --> 00:07:21.729
Diego and Wendy were stuck inside
that old nursing home turned prison,

00:07:21.771 --> 00:07:26.776
waiting for the legal process
to slowly grind forward.

00:07:27.777 --> 00:07:29.612
Just one when he arrived,

00:07:29.654 --> 00:07:32.865
Diego was three by the time he got out.

00:07:34.242 --> 00:07:39.747
Eventually, he won his legal case
to stay in the United States,

00:07:39.789 --> 00:07:45.253
but not before 650 nights passed.

00:07:47.171 --> 00:07:48.339
And yet ...

00:07:49.007 --> 00:07:50.717
others are not so lucky.

00:07:52.802 --> 00:07:53.928
Kamyar Samimi ...

00:07:54.846 --> 00:07:59.559
had a green card
and 40 years in the United States,

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.937
when one day, ICE showed up at his door

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:06.524
and took him down to a private prison
in suburban Denver.

00:08:07.692 --> 00:08:10.528
Within 13 days,

00:08:10.528 --> 00:08:11.696
he was dead.

00:08:12.405 --> 00:08:14.615
The government
never got around to deciding

00:08:14.615 --> 00:08:16.325
if he should be deported.

00:08:17.285 --> 00:08:20.371
In this topsy-turvy world,

00:08:20.371 --> 00:08:25.668
we feel better when we lock up
kids with their mothers,

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:29.547
or when people don’t meet
the same end as Mr. Samimi.

00:08:31.049 --> 00:08:33.676
Is this really the best that we can do?

00:08:35.053 --> 00:08:40.266
The path to a world
that's free of immigration prisons

00:08:41.976 --> 00:08:46.064
does not begin by pretending
that migrants are perfect.

00:08:47.482 --> 00:08:49.567
It starts with a reality check.

00:08:50.568 --> 00:08:54.864
Immigration law tells us
that migrants are aliens.

00:08:56.115 --> 00:08:59.911
But we all know
that migrants are not aliens.

00:09:00.787 --> 00:09:02.789
Migrants are just people.

00:09:03.539 --> 00:09:05.458
And like most people,

00:09:05.458 --> 00:09:10.713
most of the time,
migrants are profoundly ordinary --

00:09:10.755 --> 00:09:15.343
ordinary people
asked to do the extraordinary:

00:09:15.384 --> 00:09:19.138
wage your final fight
to stay in this country

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:23.810
while you are locked up
far away from family, your friends --

00:09:23.851 --> 00:09:26.479
where lawyers are hard to come by.

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:34.195
To fix this and move toward a world
without immigration prisons,

00:09:34.195 --> 00:09:37.949
let's stop paying
CoreCivic and the GEO Group

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.409
to lock up migrants,

00:09:40.451 --> 00:09:44.413
and let's start paying lawyers
to defend them.

00:09:44.831 --> 00:09:51.796
(Cheers and applause)

00:09:53.464 --> 00:09:58.386
Having a lawyer just means that the courts
are more likely to reach

00:09:58.427 --> 00:10:01.514
a fair and a just conclusion.

00:10:02.807 --> 00:10:06.602
And when it comes
to important legal questions,

00:10:06.644 --> 00:10:08.146
let's be honest,

00:10:08.187 --> 00:10:11.315
what's more American
that a fight between lawyers?

00:10:11.357 --> 00:10:13.276
(Laughter)

00:10:13.276 --> 00:10:15.069
And we've done this in the past.

00:10:15.111 --> 00:10:18.447
The government has run
several pilot projects.

00:10:18.781 --> 00:10:23.536
Every time, support has proven enough
to get migrants out of prison,

00:10:23.578 --> 00:10:28.624
to keep them on top of their court dates
and away from trouble.

00:10:29.709 --> 00:10:32.378
But we've never let these projects grow.

00:10:33.921 --> 00:10:37.133
Politics and profits
have always stamped out

00:10:37.133 --> 00:10:39.969
the promise of freedom.

00:10:41.179 --> 00:10:43.973
We can lock up migrants,

00:10:44.015 --> 00:10:46.726
but we don't have to.

00:10:47.393 --> 00:10:51.647
Instead of hoping
that barbed wire and steel doors

00:10:51.689 --> 00:10:56.319
will guide us out of the labyrinth
of immigration law,

00:10:56.319 --> 00:10:59.447
we could invest in justice.

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:03.659
We could make sure that every migrant

00:11:03.659 --> 00:11:07.914
has a fair chance of putting
their best legal case forward.

00:11:08.998 --> 00:11:14.545
Because immigration prisons
don't get us out of a problem.

00:11:15.421 --> 00:11:18.424
Immigration prisons are the problem.

00:11:18.799 --> 00:11:25.806
(Cheers and applause)

00:11:26.015 --> 00:11:32.146
They blind us to the past,
when we did things differently,

00:11:32.188 --> 00:11:36.525
and they distract us
from the moneymaking tentacles

00:11:36.567 --> 00:11:40.488
that tie our future to the prison gates.

00:11:40.947 --> 00:11:42.740
Well, the thing is,

00:11:42.782 --> 00:11:49.705
liberty is too precious
for any of us to lose it

00:11:49.747 --> 00:11:52.750
because politicians want votes

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:56.420
and private corporations want money.

00:11:57.296 --> 00:11:58.506
Thank you.

00:11:58.506 --> 00:12:05.513
(Cheers and applause)

